<div class="art"><h1>Life Insurance for Over 50s: What Are Your Options in the UK?</h1><p class="meta">Tanweer Hussain, Protection Expert · Insurance · 9 April 2026 · 3 min read</p><p>Life insurance for over 50s in the UK is more accessible than many people assume. While premiums are higher than for a younger applicant, the majority of people over 50 can still obtain meaningful cover. Whether you have an outstanding mortgage, want to leave something for your family, or want to cover funeral costs, there are options available.</p>







<h3>1. Term Life Insurance</h3>

<p>Term life insurance pays out a lump sum if you die within a fixed period. For over-50s, this is often used to cover an outstanding mortgage or to provide a financial safety net for a spouse. You will typically need to answer medical questions, and pre-existing conditions may affect the terms offered.</p>

<h3>2. Over-50s Life Insurance Plans</h3>

<p>These are guaranteed acceptance policies, no medical questions, no health checks. The trade-off is that premiums are higher relative to the payout, and there is usually a waiting period of one to two years before the full benefit is paid. These plans are best suited to covering specific costs such as funeral expenses rather than providing significant financial protection.</p>

<h3>3. Whole of Life Insurance</h3>

<p>Whole of life policies pay out whenever you die, there is no fixed term. They cost more than term policies but provide a guaranteed payout, useful for inheritance tax planning or leaving a specific legacy.</p>

<h2>How Much Does Life Insurance Cost for Over 50s?</h2>

<p>Premiums vary significantly based on your age, health, amount of cover, and the type of policy. The best way to find accurate pricing for your specific circumstances is to speak with an adviser who can compare the full market on your behalf.</p>





<h2>Do Over 50s Need Life Insurance?</h2>

<p>Not everyone over 50 needs life insurance, but many do. Consider whether you have an outstanding mortgage, a spouse who depends on your income, children you want to provide for, or funeral costs you want to cover. An independent adviser can help you assess this honestly based on your actual circumstances.</p>



