<div class="art"><h1>Decreasing Term vs Level Term Life Insurance: What Is the Difference?</h1><p class="meta">Tanweer Hussain, Protection Expert · Insurance · 16 May 2026 · 6 min read</p><p>When you take out life insurance in the UK, one of the first decisions you will face is whether to choose decreasing term or level term cover. The distinction is straightforward but consequential: one pays a fixed lump sum throughout the policy term, while the other reduces over time to mirror a specific financial obligation. Getting this choice right from the outset means your cover is correctly matched to what you are actually trying to protect.</p>
<h2>What Is Level Term Life Insurance?</h2>
<p>Level term life insurance pays a fixed sum assured if you die within the agreed policy term. The amount your beneficiaries receive is the same whether you die in year one or year nineteen of a twenty-year policy. The monthly premium is also fixed for the duration of the term, so there are no surprises.</p>
<p>This type of cover is primarily used where the financial obligation being protected does not decrease over time. Common applications include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Replacing a salary or income for dependants over a set number of years</li>
<li>Covering an interest-only mortgage, where the outstanding balance does not reduce</li>
<li>Providing a lump sum for children’s education or future financial security</li>
<li>Covering a fixed liability such as a business loan that does not reduce in line with a repayment schedule</li>
</ul>
<h2>What Is Decreasing Term Life Insurance?</h2>
<p>Decreasing term life insurance also runs for a fixed period, but the sum assured reduces throughout the term, typically in line with the outstanding balance on a repayment mortgage. If you die in year one, the payout is close to the original sum insured. If you die in year nineteen of a twenty-year policy, the payout is much smaller, reflecting the fact that the mortgage balance has been substantially reduced by years of repayments.</p>
<p>Decreasing term policies are specifically designed for repayment mortgages. They are not suitable for interest-only mortgages, where the capital balance remains constant throughout the mortgage term.</p>

<h2>Cost Comparison</h2>
<p>As a general principle, decreasing term cover costs less per month than level term cover for the same initial sum assured and policy term. The exact difference depends on age, health, the insurer, and the specific terms of the policy. For a repayment mortgage, this makes decreasing term cover attractive because the payout is designed to track the debt, meaning you are not paying for cover that exceeds your actual need.</p>
<p>However, the cheapest option is not always the most appropriate one. If you have dependants who would also need financial support beyond the mortgage repayment, level term cover may be more appropriate even though the premium is higher.</p>
<h2>Which Is Right for You?</h2>
<p><strong>Choose decreasing term if:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>You have a repayment mortgage and your primary goal is to ensure it is paid off if you die</li>
<li>Budget is a key consideration and you want the most cost-effective mortgage protection</li>
<li>You have separate life insurance in place to cover income protection or other needs</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Choose level term if:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>You have an interest-only mortgage where the balance does not reduce</li>
<li>You want to provide a fixed lump sum for your family regardless of when you die</li>
<li>You are primarily focused on income protection rather than mortgage repayment</li>
<li>You want flexibility for your beneficiaries to use the payout as they see fit</li>
</ul>
<p>It is also possible to hold both types simultaneously. Some people take out decreasing term cover to protect their mortgage and a separate level term policy to cover income protection, business obligations, or a lump sum for their family. There is no rule against holding more than one life insurance policy.</p>
<h2>Policy Term Considerations</h2>
<p>Whichever type you choose, the policy term should align with the financial obligation you are protecting. For mortgage protection, the term should match the remaining term of your mortgage. For income protection, the term is often chosen to run until the youngest child is financially independent, or until the policyholder’s intended retirement age.</p>
<p>Both types of policy pay out a lump sum if you die within the term. Neither has a cash value at the end if no claim is made, and neither provides any return on premiums paid. They are pure protection products, not savings or investment vehicles.</p>


<h3>Can I change from decreasing term to level term part way through my policy?</h3>
<p>Changing the type of policy mid-term is not generally possible. If your circumstances change and you need a different type of cover, you would need to take out a new policy. A broker can help you assess whether it makes sense to keep your existing policy and add a new one alongside it.</p>
<h3>Does decreasing term insurance track my exact mortgage balance?</h3>
<p>Decreasing term policies reduce according to a fixed schedule built into the policy, typically matching a standard repayment mortgage at a specified interest rate. They do not read your actual mortgage balance in real time. If your mortgage rate or term changes significantly, it is worth reviewing whether your cover still aligns with your outstanding debt.</p>
<h3>Is decreasing term insurance suitable for an interest-only mortgage?</h3>
<p>No. On an interest-only mortgage, the capital balance does not reduce during the mortgage term, so a decreasing sum assured would leave a shortfall. Level term insurance is the appropriate choice for an interest-only mortgage, as the payout remains constant throughout the term.</p>
<h3>What happens if the decreasing term policy pays out less than the remaining mortgage balance?</h3>
<p>This could occur if the policy was not set up to correctly match the mortgage from the start, or if the mortgage has been restructured. It is important to ensure that the initial sum assured and the reduction schedule are appropriate for your specific mortgage when the policy is taken out. A broker can help ensure the two are properly aligned.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<ul>
<li>Association of British Insurers (ABI)</li>
<li>Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)</li>
<li>GOV.UK</li>
</ul>

<p>About the Author</p>


<p class="disc">This article is for general information only and does not constitute financial advice or a personal recommendation. Protection policies have no cash-in value at any time and cover will cease if premiums are not maintained.</p></div>
<div class="art"><h1>Can You Have Two Life Insurance Policies in the UK?</h1><p class="meta">Tanweer Hussain, Protection Expert · Insurance · 19 May 2026 · 6 min read</p><p>There is no legal restriction in the UK on how many life insurance policies you can hold at any one time. Individuals regularly hold two or more policies simultaneously, and this is entirely permitted by UK law and by the insurance industry’s regulatory framework. The more relevant question is not whether you can hold multiple policies, but why you might want to and what you need to disclose when applying.</p>
<h2>Why People Hold Multiple Life Insurance Policies</h2>
<p>There are several legitimate and common reasons why someone might take out a second or subsequent life insurance policy:</p>
<p><strong>Filling a coverage gap:</strong> If your circumstances have changed significantly since you took out your original policy, a second policy can be used to top up the level of cover without replacing the existing arrangement. For example, if you have had children since taking out your first policy, you may want additional cover to protect them.</p>
<p><strong>Different policy terms and purposes:</strong> You might hold a decreasing term policy to cover your repayment mortgage and a separate level term policy to pay a portion of your income for your family. These serve different purposes and are structured differently, so holding both simultaneously is entirely sensible.</p>
<p><strong>Trust arrangements:</strong> Where a policy is written in trust for specific beneficiaries, taking out a second policy allows you to direct different sums to different people or purposes. For example, one policy might be written in trust for your children and another for a business partner as part of a shareholder protection arrangement.</p>
<p><strong>Business protection:</strong> Business owners sometimes hold personal life insurance alongside business-related cover such as key person insurance or shareholder protection. These serve different economic purposes and are held separately.</p>
<p><strong>Group scheme plus personal policy:</strong> Many employed people have death-in-service benefit provided through their employer, which typically pays a multiple of salary. They may also hold a personal policy to supplement this cover, providing additional security that is not dependent on their employer or their continued employment.</p>
<h2>How Insurers Assess Total Cover at Application</h2>
<p>When you apply for a new life insurance policy, the application form will typically ask whether you have existing life insurance in place. You are expected to disclose this honestly. Insurers ask because they want to understand your total level of life cover relative to your income, debts, and dependants.</p>
<p>There is no precise industry-wide rule on the maximum permissible total cover, but insurers apply what is known as the principle of insurable interest. Life insurance is intended to cover a genuine financial loss, not to generate a profit. Most insurers apply a multiple of your annual income as a rough benchmark for the total cover they are prepared to accept. If the combined cover across all policies appears disproportionate to your financial circumstances, an insurer may ask for further information or limit the additional cover they offer.</p>
<p>Failing to disclose an existing policy when asked to do so on an application could constitute a material non-disclosure, which could affect the validity of the policy if a claim is made. Honest and complete disclosure is always the correct approach.</p>
<h2>No Legal Cap on the Number of Policies</h2>
<p>The FCA does not impose a legal maximum on the number of life insurance policies an individual may hold. Each insurer sets its own underwriting limits, and these are applied at the point of application. Provided you disclose your existing cover accurately and the total is proportionate to your financial position, there is no rule preventing you from holding multiple policies.</p>
<h2>Practical Considerations When Holding Multiple Policies</h2>
<ul>
<li>Keep records of all policies, including the insurer, policy number, sum assured, and term. Beneficiaries need to be able to locate and claim on all relevant policies in the event of your death.</li>
<li>Review your policies periodically to ensure the combined cover remains appropriate to your current circumstances.</li>
<li>Consider whether each policy should be written in trust. Policies not written in trust may form part of your estate and could attract inheritance tax.</li>
<li>If you hold multiple policies for different purposes, ensure your beneficiaries understand which is which and what each is intended to cover.</li>
</ul>


<h3>Do I need to tell a new insurer about my existing life insurance?</h3>
<p>Yes. Most life insurance applications specifically ask whether you have existing policies. You must answer this honestly. Non-disclosure of material information, including existing cover, can give an insurer grounds to void a policy and refuse a claim. Always disclose all existing policies accurately.</p>
<h3>Will having multiple policies affect my premium?</h3>
<p>The existence of other policies does not in itself typically affect the premium on a new policy. Premiums are based on your age, health, lifestyle, the sum assured, and the policy term. However, if the total cover across all your policies is very high relative to your income, an insurer may ask questions or limit the additional sum they will insure you for.</p>
<h3>Can I have a personal policy and death-in-service benefit at the same time?</h3>
<p>Yes. Death-in-service benefit provided through an employer scheme and a personal life insurance policy are entirely compatible. Many people hold both. Death-in-service benefit is worth checking, as it is typically not written in trust separately and is often at the discretion of the scheme trustees, meaning it is not guaranteed to pass to your intended beneficiaries.</p>
<h3>Is it possible to claim on two life insurance policies at the same time?</h3>
<p>Yes. If you hold two or more valid policies, all of them can be claimed on simultaneously in the event of your death, provided all claims are legitimate and the policies were taken out with proper disclosure. There is no restriction on making multiple claims, as life insurance does not operate on the same indemnity principle as, for example, home insurance.</p>
<h2>Sources</h2>
<ul>
<li>Association of British Insurers (ABI)</li>
<li>Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)</li>
</ul>

